3.
Scenario:
You are tasked with creating a web page for a community center that hosts weekly activities. The community center wants to display a schedule table for their visitors. Some activities span multiple time slots, and you need to use the rowspan attribute to represent this visually in the table.

Instructions:
File Setup:
Create a new HTML file and name it community_schedule.html.

Create a Schedule Table:
Use only basic HTML to create a table representing the activity schedule. Do not use any CSS.

Table Structure Requirements:

The table must have 7 rows for each day of the week (Monday to Sunday).
It should have 4 columns:
Day
Time
Activity
Location
Use the rowspan attribute where an activity spans multiple time slots.
Activity Details: Use the following schedule (or create your own):

Day           Time             Activity   Location
Monday 8:00 AM - 9:00 AM Yoga         Room A
9:00 AM - 11:00 AM Painting Workshop Room B
Tuesday 10:00 AM - 12:00 PM Cooking Class (2 hrs) Kitchen
Wednesday 8:00 AM - 9:00 AM Meditation Room C
Thursday 1:00 PM - 3:00 PM Dance Class (2 hrs) Studio
        9:00 AM - 12:00 PM Photography Basics Room D
Note: Ensure that the rowspan attribute is used for activities spanning multiple time slots.

Additional Requirements:

Add a meaningful <caption> for the table.
Use <thead> for the table headers (Day, Time, Activity, Location).
Use <tfoot> to add a note, e.g., "Schedule is subject to change."
File Submission:

Save your file with the name community_schedule.html.
Ensure the HTML is properly indented and includes comments explaining how rowspan is applied.

4.

 Project Team and Task Management
Scenario:
You are working as a web developer for a software company. Your manager has asked you to create a web page that displays the team structure for a project, including each team member's tasks. To organize the information, you must use a nested table to display tasks assigned to each team member.

Assignment Instructions:
File Setup:
Create an HTML file named team_tasks.html.

Project Team Table:
Use basic HTML to create a table representing the project team and their respective tasks.

The outer table will list team members, their roles, and their assigned tasks.
The tasks for each member will be presented in a nested table.
Table Structure Requirements:

The outer table should have the following columns:
Team Member Name
Role
Tasks
Each nested table inside the "Tasks" column should have the following columns:
Task Name
Deadline
Sample Data: Use the following sample data to populate the tables:

Outer Table:

Team Member Name Role Tasks
Alice        Project Manager See nested table
Bob        Developer See nested table
Carol          Tester See nested table

Nested Tables for Tasks:

Alice (Project Manager):

Task Name        Deadline
Prepare Project Plan 2025-01-20
Client Communication 2025-01-22


Bob (Developer):

Task Name        Deadline
Develop Login Module 2025-01-25
Fix Bug #1234        2025-01-27

Carol (Tester):

Task Name      Deadline
Test Login Module 2025-01-28
Create Test Report 2025-01-30
Additional Requirements:

Add a <caption> to the outer table: "Project Team and Task Overview."
Add a <caption> to each nested table: "Tasks Assigned to [Team Member Name]."
Ensure the HTML is properly indented for readability.
Do not use CSS.